        z = 2.5:  a0 tied to rho_Lambda -> Delta log a0 = +0.000 dex;   a0 tied to H(z) -> Delta log a0 = +0.576 dex
        z = 5.0:  a0 tied to rho_Lambda -> Delta log a0 = +0.000 dex;   a0 tied to H(z) -> Delta log a0 = +0.919 dex
  [FAIL] A7 [observability] a tie to a strictly constant rho_Lambda is falsifiable within one universe by something other than the value of kappa itself   (it is not: a constant times a constant is a constant.  A theory must therefore state WHICH quantity a0 tracks.  Tied to rho_Lambda the only handle is the coefficient (Section D: not resolvable at 8%); tied to H(z) the deep-MOND Tully-Fisher zero point moves +0.576 dex by z = 2.5, which IS decisive.  NECESSARY CONDITION N7 -- and it is why the programme's registered a0(z) measurement, not the coefficient, is the discriminating observable.)

