SECTION B -- the zero-mode obstruction, generalised: is k01's result a property of that action or a theorem?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    B1: k01 statics -- equations invariant under J -> J + C: True;  k01 FLRW -- Lambda_eff = -J_0*K_B/2 + J_0 + K_0/2 + Lambda
  [PASS] B1 [CONTROL] the general zero-mode argument, applied to k01's own action, reproduces k01's K1 (statics: J enters only through J') and K2 (Lambda_eff = Lambda + (2-K_B) J(0)/2 + K(Q0)/2)   (both reproduced independently in this script)
    B2: F1  J(Y), Y = phidot^2/a^2 (one field, generic J)    -> additive constant absorbed by a shift of Lambda: True
    B2: F2  J(Y) + W(Y)^2 (a squared primitive)              -> additive constant absorbed by a shift of Lambda: True
    B2: F3  J(Y) with a second field's gradient mixed in     -> additive constant absorbed by a shift of Lambda: True
    B2: F4  J(Y) x an explicit numerical coefficient 7/3     -> additive constant absorbed by a shift of Lambda: True
  [PASS] B2 [THEOREM] for every local Lagrangian tested in which the MOND function multiplies the metric volume element with a field-independent coefficient, the additive constant is EXACTLY a shift of Lambda in every field equation   (the shift adds C sqrt(-g), which IS a cosmological-constant term; the result does not depend on the form of the function, on the number of fields, or on the coefficient.  k01's K1/K2 are corollaries.  OBSTRUCTION O1, generic.)
    B3: (i)  chi-equation difference under J -> J+C at shifted Lambda: -C*a(t)**3*Derivative(h(chi(t)), chi(t))  (nonzero and independent of the Lambda shift: NOT a zero mode)
    B3: (ii) with an independent measure the equations are invariant in FORM under J -> J+C: True; the psi equation integrates to J + C = M, so C is absorbed into an INTEGRATION CONSTANT M -- undetermined by the action, not derived
    B3: (iii) a total-derivative term leaves every bulk equation unchanged: True -- it cannot fix the constant either (it fixes nothing in the bulk)
    B3: (iv) with a0 = beta sqrt(G) q the 'constant' term is b beta^2 q^2: the q-equation sees it, and the Legendre energy eps = q P_q - P = q**2*(Z/2 + b*beta**2) carries it with a PLUS sign (k04's F1, re-derived)
  [PASS] B3 [exceptions] at least one escape from O1 removes the additive freedom rather than relocating it   (exactly one does: (iv), promoting a0 to a dynamical amplitude.  (i) makes the constant a POTENTIAL for another field -- it removes the degeneracy with Lambda but replaces it with an undetermined function h; (ii) converts the Lagrangian parameter into an INTEGRATION CONSTANT (unimodular/two-measure), which is initial data, not a prediction; (iii) is invisible to the bulk equations.)
  [FAIL] B4 [verdict] the additive-constant zero mode is NOT generic to local Lagrangians (there is a local, metric-measure, field-independent-coefficient counterexample)   (it IS generic.  THEOREM T1: in any local action whose MOND function F multiplies sqrt(-g) with a field-independent coefficient, F -> F + C adds C sqrt(-g) and is exactly degenerate with Lambda; the field equations depend on C only through Lambda_eff.  The degeneracy is broken if and only if the additive constant fails to multiply the metric volume element alone -- and of the four ways that can happen, only the promotion of a0 to a dynamical amplitude turns the constant into a determined quantity.)

