SECTION D -- the coefficient: what precision a theory would need, and what class of number is in play
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    D1: combined kappa = 0.5301 +/- 0.0374 (7.1%); the two measurements differ by +0.086 +/- 0.087 = 0.98 sigma (consistent)
    D1: 3-sigma statistical band on kappa: [0.418, 0.642]
  [PASS] D1 [combination] the two kappa measurements are mutually consistent, so a single combined value is meaningful   (0.98 sigma apart; combined 0.530 +/- 0.037)
    D2: kappa_meas = a0/(c sqrt(G rho_Lambda)) ~ 1/H0 at fixed Omega_Lambda: Planck -> SH0ES moves kappa by -7.7%
    D2: statistical 7.1% + H0-convention half-range 3.8% -> total 8.0%; 3-sigma band with convention [0.402, 0.658]
  [FAIL] D2 [convention] the H0-convention systematic on kappa is small compared with the statistical error, so a theory can be tested against the Planck value alone   (it is 3.8% against 7.1% statistical -- comparable.  A theoretical prediction of kappa is only testable if the H0 convention is STATED with it, and the total comparison uncertainty is 8.0%: nothing inside [0.40, 0.66] can be rejected at 3 sigma today.)
    D3: catalogue of 71 principle-shaped numbers (45 reduced rationals p/q with q <= 12, plus 26 named pi / e / phi / sqrt forms)
